# 209 — aarch64 smoke: a boot line checked whole can be split by another service's line — done

**Type:** porting (tests) · **Owner:** porting track · **Priority:** P2 · **Status:** done (2026-10-06) · **Blocked by:** — · **Roadmap:** track H · **Constitution:** MC-12.1, MC-12.2

Reported by the tools track (2026-10-06) in `issues/porting-track-reports.md`.

## Problem

`tests/aarch64_smoke.py` requires whole lines such as `[INIT] STARTED logd` in the boot output. Init and the services print to the PL011 from several CPUs at once, and a line can arrive cut by another one. One run on 2026-10-06 (tools branch, 0d49eb8) read `[INIT] STARTED [LOGD] READY` followed by the rest of init's line, and failed. The same commit passed on a second run.

## Plan

- Check the lines the test needs in the services' logs (`logs <pid>` reads one task's lines whole), as the QEMU suites do with `service_logs`.
- Or keep a task's line whole on the console: the kernel writes one task's console line at a time.

## Acceptance criteria

The boot check passes when lines interleave, with a test that interleaves them on purpose.

## Related

[201](201-aarch64-boot.done), [204](204-aarch64-profile-and-ci.done).


## Resolution

The cut came from `println!`: it wrote the formatted text and the newline in separate `LOG` calls (and `{}` arguments in more), and the kernel mirrors each call to the PL011 whole, under its lock, so another task's line could land between them. `println!` now formats into a line buffer (`libmind/src/line.rs`, 256 bytes; a longer line goes out in pieces of that size) and writes the line with its newline in one call (`process::log_line`); the system log capture and a launcher's output channel get the whole line too. Tests: host test `tests/line_host.rs` (a formatted line is one write; two writers printing piece by piece keep their lines whole; long lines in pieces), in both host test lists; `tests/aarch64_smoke.py` also checks that no line lands inside init's `[INIT] STARTED` line; three runs passed. The other plan item, checking lines in services' logs, was not needed: the disk of this smoke test has no shell.
